---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout
  title="Rock Paper Magic — LluckyGames"
  description="A combat resolver built on the rock-paper-scissors counter triangle: HP, mana, spells, and an AI you can learn to read."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <header>
      <p class="faint"><a href={`${base}/projects/`}>← Projects</a></p>
      <h1>Rock Paper Magic</h1>
      <p class="muted">
        Rock-paper-scissors, but every choice has a price and a consequence.
      </p>
      <p><span class="pill in-progress">In progress</span></p>
    </header>

    <div class="game-frame">
      <p
        style="display:flex;height:100%;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--text-faint);font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:0.9rem;padding:1rem;text-align:center"
      >
        Playable build lands here — coming soon.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>The design question</h2>
    <p>
      Pure rock-paper-scissors is a solved, luck-dominated game — there's no
      skill expression beyond guessing. But add <strong>resources</strong> (HP,
      mana) and <strong>asymmetric costs</strong> (a spell is stronger than a
      basic strike but costs mana you regenerate slowly), and the same three
      counters become a bluffing game with tempo. When do I spend mana? Can I
      read their pattern before they read mine?
    </p>

    <h2>Core loop</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Both sides pick an action <em>simultaneously</em> each round.</li>
      <li>
        Three basics — <strong>Strike</strong>, <strong>Guard</strong>,
        <strong>Feint</strong> — in the classic counter triangle, free to play.
      </li>
      <li>
        Two spells with mana costs that break or bend the triangle (e.g. a
        pierce that ignores Guard, a drain that trades damage for mana).
      </li>
      <li>Win the exchange → deal damage. Both survive → mana regenerates.</li>
      <li>First to zero HP loses. Best-of-5 rounds keeps sessions short.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Scope (deliberately tiny)</h2>
    <p>
      This is a <em>combat resolver</em>, not an RPG. No menus, no art — color,
      text, numbers. The AI has three difficulty behaviors (random, patterned,
      adaptive) precisely so the player can <em>learn to read it</em>: that
      readable progression is the actual product.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it lives</h2>
    <p>
      Source in
      <code>games/rock-paper-magic/</code> (Vite + TypeScript, no engine). The
      build drops into this page as an iframe — no install, no account, one
      click to play.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
